# Activity status by municipio, sex and age (Mexico 2020)

How `data/Mexico_data/activities/school/MX_activity_status_municipio.csv` is
produced, and why each decision was taken. Built by
`data/Mexico_data/source/build_activity_status.py`.

**This supersedes [work_status.md](work_status.md)** and the file it documents.
That file split the population by work alone, and its own closing section said
why that was wrong and what should replace it. This is the replacement.

## 1. Why this file exists

`work_status.md` established that Mexico cannot use England's rule — give every
18-64 adult a workplace — because DENUE structurally cannot host street traders,
farm labourers or unpaid family workers. It split the population into `employee`
/ `own_account` / `not_working` and that split still stands; it is reproduced
here unchanged.

What it could not express is the population under 18. Of 1,292,986 working 12-17
year olds, **38.6% also attend school**, and the two groups are not variants of
each other:

| national 12-17 workers | median hours/week | ≤20h |
|---|---|---|
| also attending school | **15h** | 69.1% |
| not attending school | **45h** | 16.6% |
| adults 18+, for comparison | 48h | 12.2% |

Working students are part-time — 15 hours, two thirds at or under 20. Working
non-students are at 45 hours against an adult median of 48: they are
school-leavers doing adult jobs, not part-timers. Half a million children hold
both a school place and a job; roughly 800,000 more have left school for
full-time work. Collapsing either into the other misrepresents both, and until a
school layer existed there was nowhere to put the distinction.

> The medians here are computed with `HORTRA = 999` (not specified, 89,455
> weighted adults) excluded. `work_status.md` reports 40h and 45h for the latter
> two rows; those figures do not reproduce and should be read from here.

## 2. The source

**Censo 2020, Cuestionario Ampliado, person records.**

| column | meaning | values |
|---|---|---|
| `ASISTEN` (`V344`) | attends school | `1` Sí, `3` No, `9` NE |
| `CONACT` (`V362`) | condition of activity | employed set is `{10,13,14,15,16,18,20}` |
| `SITTRA` (`V364`) | position in occupation | `1`..`6`, `9` = not specified |
| `SEXO`, `EDAD`, `FACTOR` | sex, age, expansion weight | |

`ASISTEN` is asked of everyone **3 and over**; `CONACT` only of those **12 and
over**. The bands below 12 therefore reach only `student` and `inactive`, which
is a property of the instrument, not a gap in the table.

`CONACT = 15` is worth noting for what it reveals about the instrument: it means
the person declared themselves a student and verification recovered that they
also work. 736,331 people weighted, 692,366 of whom attend school. The census
went looking for exactly this population.

## 3. Interpretation

### Six states, from crossing two questions

```
student              attends, does not work
student_employee     attends AND works at fixed premises      (SITTRA 1, 4)
student_own_account  attends AND works, not at a registrable one (SITTRA 2,3,5,6)
employee             works at fixed premises, does not attend
own_account          works, not at a registrable establishment, does not attend
inactive             neither attends nor works
```

The `employee` / `own_account` line is unchanged from `work_status.md`, and the
reasoning is unchanged with it: `SITTRA 4` (patrón) counts as an employee because
DENUE's size bands are explicitly "incluyendo al dueño", so excluding owners
would leave places unfilled; `SITTRA 2/3/5/6` do not, because a jornalero works a
field and a cuenta propia works a street pitch, neither of which DENUE lists.

### One attribute, not two — because independence is measurably wrong

The alternative was a `school_status` attribute alongside `work_status`. It was
rejected on the arithmetic. Among 12-17s nationally:

```
P(student) = 80.7%     P(worker) = 9.9%
independent draw  ->  8.0% do both  ->  ~1,043,000 student workers
observed          ->                       498,986
```

Sampling the two independently produces **twice** the real number of working
students. Crossing them in one table makes the joint exact by construction.

Conditioning one attribute on the other — keying `school_status` on an
already-assigned `work_status` — would also work, and the engine supports it
(`col_type: 'direct'` resolves any person attribute,
`may/attribute_assignment/data_sources.py:667`). It was rejected because
`work_status` does not exist below age 12, so every child would present a null
key, and filters are step-level rather than per-rule, so one config cannot gate
two rules by age. A single crossed attribute has neither problem and no
ordering dependency.

### "No especificado" is dropped, and this is not a detail

`SITTRA = 9` is dropped as before — 0.06% of weighted workers (32,426). So are
`ASISTEN = 9` (177,246, 0.14%) and `CONACT = 99` (340,112, 0.27%). They fall into
no status and are omitted from `Total`, which renormalises the rest
proportionally. Carrying a seventh "unknown" status would propagate a measurement
artefact into every downstream filter.

**This is load-bearing, not hygiene.** "No especificado" means the question was
asked and not answered. Read naively, it becomes an answer: `CONACT = 99` is not
in the employed set, so it reads as *does not work*; `ASISTEN = 9` is not `1`, so
it reads as *does not attend*. The result is a person who is confidently
`inactive` on the strength of no measurement at all.

In three municipios that is **every resident**:

| municipio | records | CONACT | ASISTEN |
|---|---|---|---|
| `MX04012` Seybaplaya | 1,326 (15,299 weighted) | `99` or blank, always | `9` or blank, always |
| `MX07125` Honduras de la Sierra | 1,274 (11,648 weighted) | `99` or blank, always | `9` or blank, always |
| `MX29048` La Magdalena Tlaltelulco | — | same | same |

Inferring through it produced three municipios that were **100% inactive** —
43,590 people with no workers and no students, which is not a place. Dropping the
unspecified empties them out of the table instead, so a resident misses the
lookup and stops the build — one of the fifteen instances of [the
no-invented-values
commitment](README.md#no-invented-values-one-commitment-stated-once). That is
the correct outcome and matches how the commuting matrix already treats them.

The distinction that makes this safe is **blank versus unspecified**. A blank
`CONACT` on an under-12 means *not asked* — the census does not put them in the
labour-force universe — and is 20.21% of the population. That blank is a
legitimate not-working and is kept. Only `99` at 12+ is dropped. Same for
`ASISTEN`: blank below 3 is not asked; `9` at 3+ is unanswered.

`build_work_status.py` does **not** make this distinction, so
`MX_work_status_municipio.csv` carries the fabrication. It is one more reason
that file is superseded.

This is why the built figures sit slightly under `work_status.md`'s headline
numbers: 498,350 works-and-studies against 498,986, and 793,309 works-only
against 794,000. The difference is exactly the 1,327 `SITTRA = 9` workers aged
12-17. `studies only` (10,026,679) and `neither` (1,730,904) match to the person.

### Keyed on residence, and on age

Activity status is a property of the person, assigned before they are given a
school or a workplace, so it is keyed on **residence** municipio and a per-state
run is valid.

Bands are narrow where attendance moves fast and wide where nothing changes:

```
3   4   5   6-11   12-14   15-17   18-29   30-49   50-64   65+
```

Single years at 3, 4 and 5 are not fussiness — attendance goes 25.0% -> 69.4% ->
89.6% across those two years, the steepest movement anywhere in the table. A
single `3-5` band would put a three-year-old and a five-year-old on the same
70% draw and be wrong about both.

| band | attends | n |
|---|---|---|
| 3 | 25.0% | 2,104,791 |
| 4 | 69.4% | 2,195,606 |
| 5 | 89.6% | 2,197,616 |
| 6-11 | **94.3%** | 13,055,486 |
| 12-14 | 89.5% | 6,567,311 |
| 15-17 | **71.7%** | 6,481,931 |
| 18-29 | 22.0% | 24,601,498 |
| 30-49 | 1.8% | 34,550,654 |
| 50-64 | 0.5% | 17,517,456 |
| 65+ | 0.2% | 10,393,656 |

Attendance plateaus at 94.3% through primary, erodes from 12, and collapses
through adolescence: 78.0% at 15, 71.2% at 16, 66.2% at 17, 50.4% at 18. The
15-17 band is where a school system stops being universal, which is exactly the
band where working while studying appears.

There is no row below 3, because `ASISTEN` is not asked below 3. The consuming
config must filter on `age >= 3` for that reason — and because the engine's
categorical filter **skips** null values rather than rejecting them
(`may/attribute_assignment/assigner.py:352`), so a two-year-old with no
`activity_status` would otherwise pass a `values: [...]` filter and be given a
school place.

## 4. Transformation

```
key    = ("MX" + ENT + MUN, "male" | "female", age band)
status = student | student_employee | student_own_account
       | employee | own_account | inactive
```

Accumulate `FACTOR`, emit one row per key.

